

Signed Volume (SV) is a measure of how much the portfolio is exposed to highly traded assets. 

High SV means that the allocations is highly exposed to stocks with a lot of trading activity (i.e. broad market activity) 
Low SV means either that the portfolio is concentrated in low traded stocks or that it equally shorts liquid assets 

---> the magnitude of SV says if the allocation is long or short liquid (highly traded) stocks. Is it in hot names ! 


features idea: 

- abs(sv) : is highly exposed to liquidity 
- abs(sv) / (1 + turnover) : turnover adjusted liquidity exposures is the portfolio exposed to high liquidity stocks as a result of frequent rebalancing ?
- abs(sv) * returns : liquidity returns agreeement : positive means my bet on liquidity stocks pays out 

- market_trend * sv 


At portfolio level, one day reversal is not true 


